<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Me Agtsom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">704–755</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Tride Tsugtsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Me Agtsom, also called Tride Tsugtsen, was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> from 705 to 755, coming to the throne as an infant under the regency of his grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrimalo">Thrimalö</a>. In 710 he married the Tang princess Jincheng. His long reign saw renewed wars with the Tang in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a>, and it ended when he was killed by ministers in 755. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> succeeded him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tridé Tsuktsen was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tridu-songtsen">Tridu Songtsen</a> and Tsenma Toktokteng; his nickname Mé Aktsom, Bearded Grandfather, was given to him later in life because he was so hirsute. According to the Tibetan Annals he was born in the spring of 704, a few months before his father died in battle in the Mywa territory of Nanzhao. The sources on what followed are unclear. The historian Beckwith states that another son, Lha Balpo, took the throne in the winter of 704–705, but that the empress dowager <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrimalo">Thrimalö</a> deposed him in favour of the infant, amid revolts and executions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He was crowned in 705 but not formally enthroned until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrimalo">Thrimalö</a>'s death in 712. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-jincheng">Princess Jincheng</a>, an adoptive daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhongzong-of-tang">Emperor Zhongzong of Tang</a> and a devout Buddhist, came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in 710. Five Buddhist temples were built, and about 737 the princess gave refuge to monks fleeing an anti-Buddhist king of Khotan. After she died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> between 739 and 741, anti-Buddhist factions blamed the epidemic on the royal support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and the monks fled again.</p><p>Tibetan armies fought far beyond the kingdom's borders. With the Türgish they attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> in 717, and they struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> in 722 and 736. In 727 the king seized the Chinese fortress of Guazhou, whose stores fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a>, but the Chinese drove them out in 728. A treaty of 730 fixed the border at the Chiling Pass east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a>. The Chinese later retook the Nine Loops of the Huang Ho in 738, Anrong in 740 and Shipu in 742, and in 747 forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-xianzhi">Gao Xianzhi</a> drove the Tibetans from the region.</p><p>In 751 Tibetan troops helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and Qarluq Turks defeat the Chinese at Talas, and the same year Kag-la-bon, ruler of Nanzhao, came to pay his respects; Tibetan armies were sent to help Nanzhao against the Chinese in 754.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the pillar later erected at Zhol in front of the Potala, Mé Aktsom was murdered in 755 in a palace revolt sponsored by two ministers. He was followed by his pro-Buddhist son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a>, who was given the epithet Dharma King. Edicts of Trisong Detsen and his son Sadnalegs point to dissension and suppression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in the latter part of Mé Aktsom's reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mé Aktsom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa District</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.12&amp;year=737" aria-label="Lhasa District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6921 1080 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Me Agtsom"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7371 1350h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7371 1350h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.65,91.12&amp;year=737">What was here in 737 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
